course: dictionary
lesson: 10437
title: Trousers
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Trousers is a noun for a piece of clothing that covers your body from the waist down, with a separate part for each leg. You can see what it means from the word itself: trou-sers, one part for each leg.
explain: Trousers is always plural. You say «a pair of trousers», not «a trouser». Scissors and glasses work the same way, because each has two parts.
explain: «Keep away and wear long trousers on overgrown paths.» The context tells you trousers cover your legs, so they protect you from stinging nettles. Careful with French: «une trousse» is a pencil case, not trousers.
fact: Trousers is plural only, so say «a pair of trousers» and «school trousers».
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, trousers are part of many school uniforms.
fact: In the USA and Canada, people usually say «pants» for trousers. In the UK, «pants» means underwear, so be careful with that word.
fact: «Metal rivets on jeans were patented in 1873 to stop pockets tearing on miners' work trousers.»
fact: A trouser pocket is a pocket in a pair of trousers.
exercise: «Keep away and wear long trousers on overgrown paths.» What are trousers? | *A piece of clothing that covers your body from the waist down | A plant with tiny stinging hairs | A rubber for rubbing out pencil | A pencil case for your pens
exercise: Which is the correct way to say one piece of clothing like this? | *a pair of trousers | a trouser | a trousers | three trouser
exercise: In the USA and Canada, what do people usually say for trousers? | Trainers | *Pants | Socks | Jumpers
test: Which word completes the sentence? «I need a ___ of trousers for school.» | piece | *pair | bit | cup
test: Which word is always plural, like trousers? | scissor | glass | *scissors | news
exam: Which sentence uses trousers correctly? | I bought three trouser. | I bought a trousers of blue. | *I bought a new pair of school trousers. | I bought a new trousers.
